## Avatar Script

[INTRO - 0:00-0:35]
"Your roadmap is taking shape, but initiatives rarely exist in isolation. Some must complete before others can start. Resources are shared across multiple projects. In this video, we'll master dependency management and resource planning – the details that make your roadmap executable."

[SECTION 1: Understanding Dependencies - 0:35-1:30]
"A dependency exists when one initiative requires another to complete, partially complete, or reach a specific state before it can proceed.

For example, 'Implement Advanced Analytics' depends on 'Establish Data Governance' – you can't do sophisticated analytics without reliable data.

Dependencies can be: Finish-to-Start (most common – A must finish before B starts), Start-to-Start (B can start when A starts), Finish-to-Finish (B finishes when A finishes), or Start-to-Finish (rare, B can finish when A starts).

Identifying dependencies prevents scheduling conflicts and ensures smooth handoffs between initiatives."

[SECTION 2: Creating Dependencies - 1:30-2:45]
"In Consultify's Roadmap module, there are two ways to create dependencies.

From Timeline view, hold Shift and drag from one initiative to another. An arrow appears showing the dependency relationship. By default, this creates a Finish-to-Start dependency.

Alternatively, open an initiative's detail panel and look for the Dependencies section. Click 'Add Dependency' and select the predecessor initiative and dependency type.

The dependency arrow visually connects the initiatives on the timeline. If the predecessor moves, the dependent initiative's earliest start date adjusts automatically."

[SECTION 3: Dependency Validation - 2:45-3:45]
"Consultify validates your dependencies to catch problems. You'll see warnings for:

Circular dependencies – A depends on B which depends on C which depends on A. This is impossible and must be resolved.

Timeline conflicts – A must finish before B starts, but you've scheduled B earlier than A's finish date.

Critical path violations – Dependencies that create unrealistic timelines given your end date.

The Dependency Analyzer, accessible from the Roadmap menu, shows all dependencies in a network diagram. This is especially useful for complex roadmaps with many interconnected initiatives."

[SECTION 4: Resource Types - 3:45-4:30]
"Resource planning ensures you don't overcommit. In Consultify, you can define different resource types:

People resources: your team members, external consultants, stakeholder time.

Budget resources: capital expenditure, operational expense, specific funding pools.

Asset resources: technology platforms, environments, licenses with capacity limits.

Set up your resource catalog in Settings. For each resource, define capacity – how much is available per time period."

[SECTION 5: Assigning Resources to Initiatives - 4:30-5:30]
"Open any initiative to assign resources. In the Resources section, add the resources this initiative requires and the quantity or percentage needed.

For example: '2 FTE Data Engineers for 3 months' or 'Solution Architect at 50% allocation.'

As you assign resources, the roadmap tracks total demand against available capacity. View this in the Resource Allocation view – bars show usage over time, with capacity lines indicating limits.

When demand exceeds capacity, you'll see over-allocation highlighted in red. This signals that either the timeline needs adjustment, or additional resources are required."

[SECTION 6: Resolving Resource Conflicts - 5:30-6:30]
"When you encounter resource conflicts, you have several options:

Reschedule initiatives – Move them to periods with available capacity. Drag on the timeline or adjust dates in the initiative detail.

Adjust resource allocation – Maybe you don't need 100% of a resource; 75% might suffice.

Add capacity – Hire contractors, bring in consultants, purchase additional licenses.

Parallelize differently – If two initiatives compete for the same resource, perhaps one can start later or be split into phases.

The Resource Optimizer tool suggests resequencing options that resolve conflicts while minimizing schedule impact. Review its suggestions and accept those that make sense."

[SECTION 7: Critical Path Analysis - 6:30-7:05]
"The critical path is the longest sequence of dependent initiatives. It determines your minimum timeline – if anything on the critical path is delayed, the entire roadmap slips.

Consultify highlights the critical path in your Timeline view. Use this to focus attention on the initiatives that matter most for schedule performance.

Non-critical initiatives have float – they can slip without affecting the overall timeline. Knowing this helps you prioritize when trade-offs are necessary."

[OUTRO - 7:05-7:30]
"Dependencies and resources turn a wishful roadmap into an executable plan. Spend the time to model them correctly – it pays off in smoother execution.

With your roadmap complete, it's time to start implementation. Next, we'll cover running effective pilot programs. Execution time begins!"

